        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ज्वरः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ज्वरः (jvaraḥ) — \"a fever\". मम ज्वरः अस्ति (mama jvaraḥ asti) — \"I have a fever\", literally \"of me a fever is\", with मम and अस्ति, which you know."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Jvara is built on the root √jvar, \"to be feverish\", which MW sets beside √jval, \"to burn, blaze\" (CDIAL 5303). It lives on as Hindi and Bengali jar, \"fever\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An illness, said with of-me and is."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
